# Scavenger Hunt Selfie App — Beginner's Guide to Publishing on GitHub

This app is one file: `index.html`. Everything — the design, the camera,
the confetti — lives inside it. To put it on the internet so a QR code can
point at it, we'll use **GitHub Pages**, which is free hosting built right
into GitHub.

You don't need to know git or the command line for this — everything below
can be done by clicking around on github.com.

## Step 1 — Make a GitHub account

If you don't already have one, go to [github.com](https://github.com) and
sign up. Free plan is all you need.

## Step 2 — Create a new repository

A "repository" (repo) is just a project folder on GitHub.

1. Once logged in, click the **+** icon top-right → **New repository**.
2. Give it a name, e.g. `scavenger-hunt`. No spaces — use a hyphen if you
   need one.
3. Keep it set to **Public** (Pages needs this on the free plan).
4. Leave everything else as default. Click **Create repository**.

## Step 3 — Upload the file

1. On your new repo's page, click **Add file → Upload files**.
2. Drag in `index.html` (and `README.md` if you want it there too).
3. Scroll down, click **Commit changes**.

That's it — your file is now on GitHub.

## Step 4 — Turn on GitHub Pages

1. In your repo, click **Settings** (top menu bar of the repo, not your
   account settings).
2. In the left sidebar, click **Pages**.
3. Under "Build and deployment" → **Source**, choose **Deploy from a
   branch**.
4. Under **Branch**, choose `main` and folder `/ (root)`. Click **Save**.
5. Wait about a minute, then refresh the page. GitHub will show you a green
   box with your live URL, something like:
   `https://your-username.github.io/scavenger-hunt/`

Open that link on your phone to test it — you should see the pink
scavenger hunt page and be able to tap a box to open your camera.

## Step 5 — Make the QR code

1. Copy your GitHub Pages URL from Step 4.
2. Go to a free QR generator like [qr-code-generator.com](https://www.qr-code-generator.com)
   or [qrcode-monkey.com](https://www.qrcode-monkey.com).
3. Paste in your URL, generate, download the QR image, and print it for
   your booth.

**Important:** the camera only works over HTTPS. GitHub Pages URLs are
always HTTPS, so this is handled automatically — no extra setup needed.

## Making changes later

Whenever you want to edit wording, colors, or tasks:

1. Go to your repo on github.com, click on `index.html`.
2. Click the pencil (✏️) icon top-right to edit directly in the browser.
3. Make your changes, scroll down, click **Commit changes**.
4. GitHub Pages updates automatically within a minute or two — no need to
   redo Step 4.

### What to edit for common tweaks

- **Task wording:** search for `const TASKS =` near the top of the
  `<script>` section — each line is one task's label.
- **Colors:** search for `:root{` near the top of the `<style>` section —
  `--pink-bg`, `--ink`, `--tape` etc. control the palette everywhere at
  once.
- **Photo collage strip:** right under the opening `<body>` tag there's a
  `.photo-strip` block with four placeholder swatches. If you have rights
  to your own top-down table photos, upload the images to the repo (same
  "Add file → Upload files" as before) and swap a swatch `<div>` for
  `<img src="your-photo.jpg">`.
- **Fonts:** the title uses two custom typefaces — "UNLV PMA" is set in
  Pragmatica Bold, "SCAVENGER HUNT" in Open Sauce Sans Bold. Open Sauce is
  free and loads automatically from Fontshare. Pragmatica is a paid
  commercial font (ParaType) that can't be pulled from a free CDN — if you
  own a license, upload `Pragmatica-Bold.woff2` (and `.woff` if you have
  it) into a `fonts/` folder in the repo and it'll pick it up automatically.
  Until then, the page falls back to Archivo Black, a free lookalike.

## Notes

- Progress is kept in the browser's memory only — reloading the page resets
  it. That's fine for a single sitting at a booth. If you want progress to
  survive someone closing the tab and coming back later, let me know and
  I'll wire that in.
- If a student denies camera permission, they'll see a friendly fallback
  message instead of a blank screen.
- The pointing-hand graphics are original artwork drawn for this app (not
  copied from any source image), so they're yours to use freely.
